 ## Unresolved questions
 
-- [Q-E09](../../../BACKLOG.md#q-e09) (permanent discovery write timing) is registered on this card and is `proposed` with its product half unanswered. It shapes `profile.discoveries`, `profile.recipes` and `profile.unlocks` by deciding when an entry commits, never what is stored. This section adopts no answer and no proposal. The commit ordering itself is HF-07b's. D26 in the decision register is the same open decision and adopts no storage or write schedule.
-- [Q-P39](../../../BACKLOG.md#q-p39) (starting a new run while a run is alive) is registered on this card and is open. It decides whether a live, non-terminal run may be replaced and what durable record, if any, an abandoned run leaves. It changes no field here under either answer, as S8 records: a run is identified by `run.run_id` and started with `run.start`, and the rule that decides *when* a new run may be written is the commit-boundary card's admission rule, not a field. This section names no abandonment fact and adopts no answer.
-- [Q-P16](../../../BACKLOG.md#q-p16) (revival and unfinished-level interruption) is registered on this card and is open. Its revival half changes no field. Its interruption half is reserved as `run.interruption`, typed `object or null`, with no key named and no guarantee that it is ever non-null. TADR-004 line 32 requires spent-revival and terminal authority to survive under any selected rule, and the reservation's stated rule enforces exactly that. Moving it out of `run.checkpoint` widens where it holds rather than what it may carry: it is now readable when no level has completed, which is the case S10 walks.
