 This is a documentation contract and a reproducible paper review. It establishes no save file, no codec, no serializer, no round trip, no filesystem protocol, no recovery behaviour, no migration, no engine behaviour and no product answer. A specified stage is not an implemented stage, and a validate gate named here has never rejected anything. The four-stage ordering is fixed; the storage mechanism that would make stage 3 durable is not, and TADR-004 line 38 explicitly refuses to accept a temporary file, a rename, a checksum or a backup as proof of power-loss durability. The S0-07 and S0-08 citations are source review of historical headless runs with placeholder data on one Linux host; they establish that one prototype owner behaved as described in its nine cases, not that any production projection commits correctly, and the retained receipts were read, not rerun. S0-08 lines 97 to 101 apply in full: flush and fsync durability, directory-entry durability after rename, non-Linux rename semantics and cross-process single-writer exclusion were not asserted. The paper check proves internal consistency of one file against cited sources at one base and replays a model of the protocol, not the protocol; a passing model run is not evidence that an implementation obeys the model. No build, native or browser run, dependency change, asset admission, publication or save access occurred. Both browser proofs and their storage remain separate (TADR-004 line 42), and their tests cannot qualify this native persistence contract.
